 ## Overview
-The Differentiable Uncalibrated Imaging Framework addresses the problem of image reconstruction when the measurement coordinates (e.g., sensor locations, projection angles) are uncertain or the system is out of calibration [S1, unofficial][S2]. It formulates the problem as measurement interpolation at unknown nodes, supervised by the forward operator, by jointly optimizing a continuous measurement representation and the uncertain coordinates [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike methods that fine-tune a neural network for unknown coordinates or train on a family of operators, this framework reevaluates measurements via a continuous representation for a single example, avoiding the need for fine-tuning or handling distributional tradeoffs [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Differentiable Inverse Graphics Framework](7617-differentiable-inverse-graphics-framework.md) by introducing a specific mechanism for handling coordinate uncertainty through joint optimization of representation parameters and input coordinates.
+The Differentiable Uncalibrated Imaging Framework addresses the problem of image reconstruction when the measurement coordinates (e.g., sensor locations, projection angles) are uncertain or the system is out of calibration [S1, unofficial][S2]. It formulates the problem as measurement interpolation at unknown nodes, supervised by the forward operator, by jointly optimizing a continuous measurement representation and the uncertain coordinates [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike methods that fine-tune a neural network for unknown coordinates or train on a family of operators, this framework reevaluates measurements via a continuous representation for a single example, avoiding the need for fine-tuning or handling distributional tradeoffs [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Differentiable Inverse Rendering](7617-differentiable-inverse-rendering.md) by introducing a specific mechanism for handling coordinate uncertainty through joint optimization of representation parameters and input coordinates [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method models the measurement space as a continuous function that maps measurement coordinates to sampled measurements. It leverages the natural differentiability of implicit neural networks (neural fields) or differentiable splines with respect to their input coordinates to enable gradient-based optimization [S1, unofficial][S2].
+The method models the measurement space as a continuous function that maps measurement coordinates to sampled measurements [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the natural differentiability of implicit neural networks (neural fields) or differentiable splines with respect to their input coordinates to enable gradient-based optimization [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
